NAME
       mv - move or rename files and directories

SYNOPSIS
       mv [-i | -f | -n] [-v] [-u] SOURCE TARGET
       mv [OPTIONS] SOURCE... DIRECTORY
       mv [OPTIONS] -t DIRECTORY SOURCE...

DESCRIPTION
       mv renames SOURCE to TARGET, or moves one or more files and
       directories into DIRECTORY. Within one file system, a move only
       changes the directory entry: it is instant, and the file keeps its
       inode, owner, permissions and SELinux context. Between file systems,
       mv copies the data and then deletes the original.

       An existing TARGET file is replaced without a question, unless you
       use -i or -n. A directory is moved with everything in it; no -r is
       needed.

       Because a moved file keeps its SELinux context, a file moved into
       /var/www/html from your home directory still has the type
       user_home_t, and Apache cannot read it. Run restorecon on it, or use
       cp instead of mv, or mv -Z.

OPTIONS
       -i, --interactive
              Ask before replacing an existing file.

       -f, --force
              Never ask (cancels an earlier -i).

       -n, --no-clobber
              Never replace an existing file.

       -u, --update
              Move only when SOURCE is newer than TARGET, or TARGET is
              missing.

       -v, --verbose
              Print each move.

       -t, --target-directory DIRECTORY
              Move all sources into DIRECTORY.

       -T, --no-target-directory
              Treat TARGET as a normal file name even if it is a directory.

       -Z, --context
              Give the moved files the default SELinux context of their new
              place.

EXAMPLES
       Rename a file:

           mv report.txt report-2026.txt

       Move several files into a directory, showing each:

           mv -v *.log /var/tmp/oldlogs/

       Move a web page into place and fix its SELinux label:

           mv ~/index.html /var/www/html/
           restorecon -v /var/www/html/index.html

SEE ALSO
       cp(1), rm(1), rename(1), restorecon(8), ls(1)
